> 
> 1st question:  has anyone already dealt with this successfully?

Yes, and no... :-)

You're prolly right in guessing that the IRQ of the sound card may be
conflicting with that of the DE435; IRQ 9 seems to be popular for sound
cards in general, but it's also one of the 4 IRQ's (10,11, and 15 are the
others) used for PCI devices if present, and set by the PCI BIOS init code.

If you are booting LINUX from ARC via MILO, you could change the kernel's
setup of the IRQ's assigned to the PCI slots in arch/alpha/kernel/bios32.c
fixup code (ie in the code that inits the PIRQ registers).

The PCI "slot" that the TGA is in may currently be setup in the PIRQs with
IRQ 15, for example; simply switch whatever the TGA slot's IRQ for IRQ 9 in
the PIRQ setup code, thus giving the DE435 the non-conflicting IRQ, as the
TGA doesn't use its IRQ anyway.

There's currently no interrupt sharing capability on the Alpha side of LINUX,
so that can't be entertained as a solution.

BTW, the AS250 is a *nice* box, quite fast from all I've heard... :-)

 
> So, I'm thinking perhaps I need to compile in a driver for the sound 
> card, so that the interrupt will be allocated by it, and the PCI de4x5 
> will then go squat on a different interrupt.  It isn't 100% clear to me, 
> however, what particular incantation I should configure for the 250's 
> sound card.  It has an OPL YMF262-M, so I'm choosing OPL 2/3 support, and 
> it has a Crystal CS4231A-KL, so I'm choosing Microsoft Sound System 
